[38;5;231m⏺[39m One more fork, then the design.

  [1mThe fork:[0m what this is built with. The repo has no package.json, no build step, no dependencies.

  - [1mVanilla JS, no build[0m — ES modules loaded directly by [38;5;153mindex.html[39m. Zero install, zero config, opens by double-clicking
    the file. Costs: manual DOM updates, and a reminder badge that re-renders on a timer is exactly the kind of state
    sync that gets fiddly by hand.
  - [1mVanilla JS + Vite + Vitest[0m — same code, plus a dev server, bundling, and a real test runner. Small config cost;
    unlocks proper unit tests of the scheduler with fake timers, which is the part most worth testing.
  - [1mA framework (React/Svelte)[0m — reactive rendering solves the badge/panel sync for free. Costs a dependency tree and
    build pipeline for an app that is, at heart, a list and a timer.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m vanilla JS with Vite and Vitest. No framework — the UI is too small to earn one. But the reminder
  scheduler has genuinely testable logic (due-window boundaries, dedupe of already-fired reminders, catch-up on app open
  after being closed for a day), and I want fake-timer tests on it rather than clicking through a page.
